Q8. Data Persistence Strategy
Solved
A developer deploys a PostgreSQL pod using the standard postgres image
 without configuring any volumes. 
 The application writes customer data to /var/lib/postgresql/data. 
 If the Pod crashes and is automatically restarted by the Kubelet on the 
 same node, what is the status of the data?

Ans. The data is lost because the Kubelet creates a fresh container with 
a clean filesystem, destroying the writable layer of the old container.

Q9. Volume Selection: emptyDir vs. hostPath
Solved
You are designing a log-shipping architecture where a fluentd sidecar container needs to read log files generated by the main application container within the same Pod. The logs are temporary and do not need to survive if the Pod is deleted. Which volume type is the most appropriate and secure choice?
Ans. emptyDir volume shared between the two containers.

Q10. Default Service Account Dangers
Solved
A developer deploys a pod without specifying a serviceAccountName. It automatically uses the default Service Account. What is the standard permission level of this token in most modern Kubernetes distributions (with default RBAC)?
Ans. Discovery Access: It can usually hit /healthz and authenticate, allowing it to map out the API versions and sometimes list namespaces (Discovery).

Q2. Access Modes & Architecture
Solved
You are deploying a web application scaled to 3 replicas. You want all 3 replicas to read and write to the same shared HTML directory simultaneously. You attempt to use a standard AWS EBS (Elastic Block Store) volume. Why will this configuration fail?
Ans. AWS EBS volumes do not support the ReadWriteMany (RWX) access mode required for simultaneous write access by multiple pods.

Q3. PVC Binding Logic
Solved
A developer creates a PVC requesting 5Gi of storage with accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce] and storageClassName: fast. In the cluster, there exists a static PV with 10Gi capacity, ReadWriteOnce, and storageClassName: fast. What happens?
Ans. The PVC binds to the PV. Kubernetes allows binding to a PV that is larger than the request, treating the request as a "minimum" requirement.

Q4. Dynamic Provisioning & Zones
Solved
You have a StorageClass configured with provisioner: kubernetes.io/aws-ebs and volumeBindingMode: Immediate (the default). You create a PVC, and it immediately binds to a new PV in us-west-2a. Later, you deploy a Pod that uses this PVC, but the scheduler places the Pod on a node in us-west-2b. What is the result?
Ans. The Pod fails with FailedAttachVolume or stays Pending because EBS volumes are zonal and cannot be attached to a node in a different zone.


Q5. Data Safety & Reclaim Policy
Solved
A critical database PVC uses a StorageClass with reclaimPolicy: Delete (the default). An administrator accidentally runs kubectl delete pvc database-data. What happens to the underlying cloud storage and the data?
Ans. Both the PV object and the physical cloud storage are immediately deleted, resulting in permanent data loss.

Q6. StatefulSet Storage Pattern
Solved
You are deploying a MongoDB cluster using a StatefulSet with 3 replicas. You define a volumeClaimTemplate in the StatefulSet spec. How does Kubernetes handle the storage for these replicas?
Ans. It creates 3 distinct PVCs (e.g., data-mongo-0, data-mongo-1, data-mongo-2), each bound to its own separate PV.
